    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Social Capital Theory continues to evolve from foundational definitions by James Coleman into a robust framework that bridges micro-level interactional analyses with macro-scale societal issues. Pierre Bourdieu's concept of habitus and field dynamically critiques structuated inequality, while Robert Putnam's work on bonded versus bridging social capital illuminates the structural impacts on civic trust and democratic health. Numerical networks played a critical role in establishing the link between resource distribution and network density, Mark granovetter further examines weak ties' unique role in information diffusion.\n\nCritiques on social capital's determinants and limitations followed, with Linda Lin Costio, Robert Loury, Francis Fukuyama, and Janet Waldinger discussing the contingent effects and underlying social structures. Richard Adler's cultural capital perspective contrasts Bourdieu's version with the distinctively technical or informational definitions proposed by Leon不便 and H. Wooldridge. An emerging focus on structural holes suggests the role of brokers in network formation and market processes reinforce the importance of bridging networks in professional opportunities.\n\nThese scholarly lenses now converge to examine transformative social change, structural integration, and political action. Together, they provide a unified but nuanced understanding of how social capital functions across academic disciplines.",
      "source": "target",
