    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Social Capital Theory has evolved significantly from foundational work by James Coleman on intra-organizational resources, who provided a strictly positivist approach to credit and gains. Meanwhile, Pierre Bourdieu critiqued earlier frameworks by emphasizing habitus and field, distinguishing between cultural and social capital to explain class reproduction. Robert Putnam expanded this macro-level analysis to democratic health, whileMark Granovetter highlighted the vital role of weak ties in job mobility and information flow.\n\nThe structuralist perspective was further developed by Ronald Burt, who focused on voids and structural holes as key advantages for brokers in professional networks. Sociologist Robert Loury differentiated economic capital from social capital, noting how racialized legacies impact accumulation, while John Woolcock balanced state and social contributions to public infrastructure. Francis Fukuyama provided a broader philosophical look at human nature and trust, influencing the debate on civic peacebuilding.\n\nEthnographer Sharon Lin and others like Elizabeth Adler, who focused on occupational social capital, have refined the scope to include ethnic and gendered networks. Janet Waldinger's analysis of structural disintegration offers a critical view on how social capital survives economic shifts. By integrating Burton, Coleman, and Bourdieu, the field now presents a unified yet complex picture",
